WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.880
Back in June at AI DevCon in London,
we had a lot of great talks about skills.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.200
There are now millions of them out there,
and the honest conclusion from the stage

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.120
was that we have built

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.800
an enormous amount of something
we don't yet know how to manage.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.400
Let's first hear from Guy Podjarny,
CEO and co-founder of Tessl, who lays out

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.000
why skills deserve to be treated

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.560
as code, and sketches out the stack
forming around them.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.760
I am Guy, or Guy.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.040
I'm the CEO and founder here
at Tessl, as Simon just said,

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:34.360
and I'm excited for two days
of sharing and learning.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.720
And I do love the hallway track.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:39.000
I love the talks on it, but I just love
the conversations and the learnings.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.320
And I'd love for you to
to share some of those learnings.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.240
You're here to to collaborate,
not just to talk.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:44.920
And indeed,

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.360
I want to talk to you about sort of
why skills, I believe, are the new code.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.280
So we

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.160
founded Tessl two plus years ago,
and we did it out of the belief

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.800
that software development is transforming
from revolving around code

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:05.200
and instructions
to revolving around intent.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:08.200
Sorry. Let me try that again.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.320
Revolving around code

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.760
and implementation to revolving around
intent and instructions.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.800
And I think that's far

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.640
less controversial today. Two years ago,
we believed that there's a new dev paradigm

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.560
to be had around it, but we didn't know
quite what it would look like.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.960
And I think today we're starting to see
that sort of new development

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.720
stack come into view,
which I find quite exciting.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.800
And so we're seeing kind of a bit
of a layered software stack.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.200
At the bottom are the new primitives,
the models that we're all building on.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:39.760
Clearly, they are the new superpower

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.920
that we've received
and we're trying to build for those.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.440
On top of those are tools,
which I'll talk about some more,

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.760
which help turn models into agents,
giving them arms and legs to be able

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.880
to affect the world
and gather information.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.480
There's context
that guides those models as well.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:58.280
And then increasingly, there is a harness
that constrains the model

00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.800
or packages a lot of things together.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.480
I'll talk about these three layers
in more depth in a section,

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.560
and then harnesses compose
into factory lines that combine them

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:09.640
all and create these pipelines
and into full-on factories.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:14.520
So we also heard from James Moss,
who works on the registry at Tessl.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.720
He gets specific about how skills sprawl
actually fails

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.640
teams: building the same skill twice
without knowing, skills drifting out

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.920
of date, and nobody able to tell
whether any of them are being used at all.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.000
So how does this sprawl manifest

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.840
actually, and impact humans
and the agents that we're instructing?

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.600
So there's a couple of different
failure modes that we're seeing.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.080
The first one is overlap.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.640
So this is where you might have multiple
teams all building the same thing

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:49.840
in isolation, without realizing. Each team
might have built their own version

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.640
of the skill.
It might achieve the same thing.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:53.120
You might have

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.120
generally the same outcomes,
but it's done in a different way, right?

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.120
And you're having multiple
kind of wasted effort there.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.440
So that's overlap.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.400
Next up we often see drift.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.520
So this is where newer skills
are shipping.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.880
And teams aren't keeping up
with those newer versions.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.520
And a good example here
recently is Matt Pocock.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:14.640
Hopefully you've all heard of him.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.560
He's a great educator,
published a bunch of great skills.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.480
He has a Grill Me skill.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.200
That's where you kind of do an interview
with the agent and you kind of

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:24.480
get a shared understanding.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.080
He recently published a newer version
of that Grill skill, called Grill

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.480
with Docs, which kind of expands on it
and adds a lot more detail to it.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.400
I'm sure there's lots of folks that don't
realize that that's been released, right?

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.520
And they're still using that older version
and haven't updated.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.280
So that's drift.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.440
We also have problems around activation,
or a lack of activation, right?

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:46.040
Lots of people are producing these skills.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:47.480
Are they being used by the agents?

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:49.080
Are they being used by humans?

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.720
There's no real way to to understand that

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.720
and know that right now. You probably don't
have that visibility yet.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.360
So I mentioned this in his talk as well.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:03:59.520
This morning.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.960
You might have a skill that describes
some code or a code base, or a process

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:08.720
or even a workflow, and those two things
can quickly go out of sync,

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.040
so your skill never gets updated.
Things change.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:14.040
And in those instances,
having an outdated skill

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.280
can often be just as bad
as having no skill at all.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:19.360
Right?

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:23.520
John Groetzinger is a principal engineer
at Cisco, and he has a line

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:24.480
I've thought a lot about

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.640
since: you don't need a smarter model,
you need smarter context.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.840
He went and tested it and found mid-tier
models could do the job

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.640
once the context was right.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.400
So this might be a little controversial

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.600
for some people here, especially
maybe if you work for a frontier model

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.040
company — earmuffs — but you don't
really need a smarter model today.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.000
The models are perfectly capable of doing
what you need for business value, right?

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.360
Not talking like PhD level intelligence
and beyond, but getting business value

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.360
out of the models. It's already there
today.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:57.760
The problem is really that you need

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.760
smarter context and smarter
context engineering.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.280
And so for me, the last couple of years
I've been jumping harness to harness.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.840
I'm always chasing the latest, greatest
thing, constantly breaking my workflows.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.600
I'm sure other people can relate to this.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:14.640
And always there's so many variables
that anytime I switch to a lower model

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.560
and it breaks something, I'm immediately
like, well, that's the model's fault.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:18.680
I'm going back to Opus, right?

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.880
Because this thing that I don't
understand, it has to be the model, right?

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:25.000
But once skills came onto the scene,
it kind of changed my

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.560
perspective on things,
especially recently with the new patterns

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.560
of agentic fan out in the harness, right,
where I have my model set to Opus,

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.800
and I ask it to do this massive prompt
and it spawns 15 subagents.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.240
That cost is just really increasing,
and we're actually seeing that becoming

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:44.280
actually a concern where we had to start
limiting model use on our large enterprise

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:48.520
because of the cost of this stuff
and the fan out being a problem.

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:50.480
So I really wanted to figure out,

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.760
okay, why can't I just use Sonnet
for everything?

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:54.400
And I found out I can.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:57.640
And today, for the last couple of months,
I largely use the medium tier model.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:05:58.640
Whether that's Sonnet,

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:01.800
GPT medium reasoning —
I don't really use those high end models.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:03.720
It's very rare that I use those.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.440
Maybe for some super complex planning.
Lots of context.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:09.440
I still use them,
but I challenge my engineers to really try

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:13.040
to use that medium tier as their baseline
and only jump into the greater tier

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:13.800
when they need to.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:18.040
And the real unlock for this was skills,
because all the harnesses,

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.800
all the models are training on skills,
they're honoring skills.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.640
And so if you can lean into that,

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:25.560
your context can actually transfer
between the harnesses.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.400
And that was great for me because I can

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:31.520
I can try Claude Code, I can jump into dev
and I can jump into GitHub Copilot CLI.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.080
And if my skills work in all three of them
relatively, it works the same.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:36.640
So it's great.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:40.640
But what you need to understand
is how do you manage your context, right?

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:41.600
How do you share it?

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:42.440
Package it once

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:46.000
and then ship it to everyone,
but only have one source of management.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.880
And the real unlock for that is evals.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:52.520
And finally, Rob Willoughby
and Simon Obstbaum

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.520
from the Stanford lab,
who actually measured this:

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:59.520
500 skills, a thousand tasks,

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.680
19 combinations of model and harness.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:04.360
Their

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:08.120
most uncomfortable finding
is that more than half the time, the model

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:12.120
followed the skill's instructions,
even when the skill was not loaded at all.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:14.000
the theorem.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.560
I want to talk a little bit
about what kind of task

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.320
and what I mean by full completion
and instruction

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:24.280
following. So what we're running
is we've got 500 skills.

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:25.360
We have 1000 tasks.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.080
We've got 19,
19 permutations of models and harnesses.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.600
So we got 19 permutations
of different models, different harnesses,

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:37.480
because those have an effect on each other
and also affect the performance.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:42.640
And then the tasks that we're using,
those are synthetic.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.880
But they're anchored on the skill itself,
they're meant to be something

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.560
that you could expect
the skill to trigger for.

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:52.640
So if you have a skill that says,
how do you implement

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:55.720
API security, the task
that we're going to construct is that

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:57.200
great.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:02.120
Or change the authentication from password
to some other mechanism for that,

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.560
or something else
that might not be related,

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:08.920
to expect them to be picking up
on the security and looking for that.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.320
And so hopefully potentially
not trying to do

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.320
like super hard pushing boundaries here.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:19.840
Think of it as kind of like well scoped
to your tickets and expecting engineers

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:21.160
to be getting that one.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:23.560
And so we see that

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:25.880
hitting a threshold of 1,993% of us,

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:30.840
whether it's in their office in holding,
this is the metrics

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.240
that are specifically in the agenda,
what they do.

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:38.520
So if you have your own internal design
for how you want to do whatever you hold

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:41.760
or to make sure that you're updating
two supervision versus another,

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:44.040
that's the information
that we put in the skill

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:47.720
and then encoded in the instruction
following improvement that we see there.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.760
One really interesting thing
about that number specifically

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:54.680
is that we see 55%
following the instructions of the skill,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.800
even when the skill is not present.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.520
That means the information
that is encoded in the skill,

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.000
that's
actually in the weights of the model already.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.320
And so when you're talking to you,
it was getting rid of anyway.

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:08.480
So that means that's actually valuable
because burning inference

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:11.600
tokens and paying money down in Google

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.960
when the model is going to get in ways
that they can get to do that.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.160
So you're giving this will be finishing.

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:21.600
But the things that you care about,
the structure of these skills

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.920
or changes, how it does it
and how well it does it.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:27.080
Thanks for watching.

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:31.720
Be sure to join us for the next
AI DevCon this November in New York City.

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:35.680
Visit AI DevCon to learn
